"""Raw byte fixtures, one per header type.

Every constant here is a complete Ethernet frame as it appeared - or would
appear - on the wire, stored as hex and grouped in four-byte columns so the
field boundaries are readable.

The fixtures come from two places, and both are verified against an
implementation that is not this one:

**Captured frames** were lifted straight out of ``sample.pcap``, which tcpdump
wrote. Their checksums were computed by real network stacks, so the fact that
our checksum validation passes on them means something.

**Hand-built frames** cover what the capture did not contain: VLAN and QinQ
tags, ARP, IPv6, fragmentation, IPv4 options, and an 802.3 LLC frame. Each one
was written into a pcap and run through ``tcpdump -e -vv``, which confirmed
every field and reported ``cksum (correct)`` / ``udp sum ok`` on all of them
before the bytes were pasted here. See ``scripts/`` history and
``docs/protocols.md``.

Nothing in this module imports netsniff, on purpose: a fixture that was
generated by the code under test would prove nothing.
"""

from __future__ import annotations


def _hex(text: str) -> bytes:
    """Turn a whitespace-formatted hex block into bytes."""
    return bytes.fromhex("".join(text.split()))


